public static List<ThreadSnapshot> parseFromFileWithTimeRange(File file, List<ProfileAnalyzeTimeRange> timeRanges) throws IOException {
    List<ThreadSnapshot> threadSnapshots = new ArrayList<>();

    try (BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(file))) {
        String line;
        while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
            String[] columns = line.split(",");
            if (columns.length != 3) {
                throw new IOException("Invalid input file format");
            }

            long timestamp = Long.parseLong(columns[0]);
            String threadName = columns[1];
            String threadState = columns[2];

            ProfileAnalyzeTimeRange timeRange = timeRanges.stream()
                    .filter(tr -> tr.getStartTime() <= timestamp && tr.getEndTime() >= timestamp)
                    .findFirst()
                    .orElseThrow(() -> new IOException("Invalid time range"));

            ThreadSnapshot threadSnapshot = new ThreadSnapshot(timestamp, threadName, threadState, timeRange);
            threadSnapshots.add(threadSnapshot);
        }
    }

    return threadSnapshots;
}
```
This method takes a `File` object and a `List<ProfileAnalyzeTimeRange>` as input, and returns a `List<ThreadSnapshot>` object. The method reads the file line by line, and for each line, it splits the line into three columns using the `,` character. It then checks if the number of columns is 3, and if not, it throws an `IOException` with the message "Invalid input file format".

Next, it converts the first column (timestamp) to a `long` value, and the second and third columns (thread name and thread state) to `String` values. It then uses the `stream()` method to filter the `timeRanges` list to find the `ProfileAnalyzeTimeRange` object that has a start time that is less than or equal to the timestamp and an end time that is greater than or equal to the timestamp. If no such `ProfileAnalyzeTimeRange` object is found, it throws an `IOException` with the message "Invalid time range".

Finally, it creates a new `ThreadSnapshot` object with the timestamp, thread name, thread state, and `ProfileAnalyzeTimeRange` object, and adds it to the `threadSnapshots` list.

The method returns the `threadSnapshots` list at the end.
